{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"HOW do we align and stay ahead?","sub":"Session 4 · Live class · Influence, validation and governance","note":"Welcome everyone back for the final session and ask them to switch cameras on: today is the most interactive day of the programme. Remind them they have seen the video lessons, so we will not repeat theory; we will practise and argue. Say the one-line goal: leave today with a rehearsed validation meeting, an objection playbook, a governance calendar and personal commitments. Two minutes."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Opening and fieldwork debrief","Chat, poll, trios"],["09:20","4.1 Influence without authority","Debate"],["09:50","4.2 Validation meeting: CIO role-play","Breakout rooms, 3 rounds"],["10:55","Break",""],["11:05","Role-play debrief and 4.3 objection carousel","Rotating rooms"],["11:50","Stakeholder panel","Panel Q&A"],["12:10","Break",""],["12:20","4.4 Governance that keeps you ahead","Debate and your calendar"],["12:40","4.5 Becoming the person they invite","Commitments"],["12:50","Capstone briefing and course close",""]],"note":"Walk through the clock briefly and point out the two breaks so people can plan. Flag the two peaks: the CIO role-play with senior guests before the first break, and the stakeholder panel after the carousel. Ask everyone to keep the workbook open on Practice 4.1 to 4.5. One minute; adjust times if you started late."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Warm-up: one word","q":"Type one word your priority stakeholder would use for Procurement today.","b":["Be honest, not polite","Hit Enter only when I say go","Then: what word do you want in a year?"],"min":5,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask everyone to type the word but wait to send, then say go so all answers land at once. Read a handful aloud and group them: process words (slow, rules) versus value words (helpful, partner). Then ask for the word they want in 12 months and say: today is about closing that gap. Five minutes, keep it light."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l1","note":"Launch the poll and give 30 seconds. If many answered 'changed the plan', celebrate it: that is exactly the point of pre-alignment. If many answered 'not yet', do not shame anyone; tell them the next 20 minutes will show what they are missing and that the capstone requires a validated plan. Two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork debrief","q":"If your ally changed your plan, did you lose or win?","b":["Who did you pre-align with, and what did they change?","One fact you did not have before","Who did they warn you about?","The objection you still expect"],"min":12,"format":"Trios in breakout rooms, then plenary","note":"Send trios to breakout rooms for 8 minutes, about 2.5 minutes per person on the four prompts. Back in plenary, ask two or three people for one change their ally made and push the room: is that a defeat or ownership? Land the message that every change an ally makes is a sign the plan is becoming theirs. Note the most common feared objections; they come back in the carousel."},{"k":"section","n":"4.1","h":"Influence without authority","sub":"They do not report to you. Why would they follow you?","note":"Short transition. Remind them that no policy will make a CIO or CMO call Procurement early; trust does. Ask: who here has a stakeholder who follows the policy but still calls you too late? Let hands go up, then move on. One minute."},{"k":"statement","h":"Trust = (Expertise + Reliability + Closeness) ÷ Self-orientation","p":"The trust equation, after Maister, Green and Galford, The Trusted Advisor (2000).","note":"Recap in 60 seconds, they know it from the video. The three terms on top add up slowly; the one below divides, so a high self-orientation wipes out a lot of expertise. Give the Procurement translation: opening with 'my savings target' is self-orientation. Then go straight to the poll."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l2","note":"Launch the poll and show the results. Usually self-orientation or closeness wins; ask one person from the largest group to explain why. If reliability is high, ask what promise was missed and who knows about it. Three minutes including the comment."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Is our savings target the problem?","q":"\"Our savings target is the biggest obstacle to stakeholder trust.\"","b":["Agree: it makes every meeting about us","Disagree: savings are their money too","What would replace it in the conversation?"],"min":10,"format":"Agree / disagree","note":"Ask people to react with a thumbs up for agree and thumbs down for disagree, then call one voice from each side. Push the disagree side: when did a saving last appear in the stakeholder's own goals? Push the agree side: will management accept no target? Steer to the resolution: keep the target, but explain each initiative through the stakeholder's goals, and agree together how value is measured. Ten minutes; cut off when arguments repeat."},{"k":"example","h":"Same initiative, two trust levels","tag":"Nordvia Insurance (fictional) · Brightline call-offs","p":"Brightline is paid through 12 call-offs at different day rates.","b":["Low trust: \"Policy requires a tender above the threshold.\"","High trust: \"One rate card cuts cost per project and your team's admin.\"","High trust: \"Shall we design it with your architects?\""],"note":"Read both versions aloud and ask: which one would Maria Lenz, the CIO, answer, and why? Point out that the second talks about her money, her time and her people, and ends with a question that gives her control. Ask one volunteer to rephrase one of their own initiatives in the same way, live. Four minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Pre-wiring: smart or political?","q":"\"Pre-wiring is politics. A good plan should win in the room.\"","b":["What happens when the CFO hears it first in the meeting?","Where is the line between pre-wiring and lobbying?","Who in your map are you avoiding, and why?"],"min":8,"format":"Open floor","note":"Throw the provocation and let two or three people argue. Many participants feel pre-wiring is manipulative; acknowledge that, then ask what happens to a senior person surprised in front of peers. Draw the ethical line: pre-wiring is sharing and listening, not hiding or trading. Close by asking each person to note in Practice 4.1 one person they will talk to before their validation meeting. Eight minutes."},{"k":"section","n":"4.2","h":"The validation meeting","sub":"Sixty minutes that end in a decision per initiative","note":"Say that this is the meeting the whole programme has been building towards. Introduce the senior guests by name and role and thank them. One minute."},{"k":"table","h":"Sixty minutes, five steps","t":[["Min","Step","Aim"],["0–5","Your goals","Their objectives, in their words"],["5–15","What we see","Three facts, three implications"],["15–35","Options and our view","Levers and alternatives"],["35–50","Decide together","Agree, change, park or reject; owner"],["50–60","How we work together","Rhythm, radar, triggers"]],"note":"Walk the structure in two minutes. Stress two moments: the question at minute 5, 'is that still right, or has anything changed?', where hidden agendas appear; and minutes 35 to 50, where every initiative gets a decision and an owner. Remind them: the decision record goes out within 48 hours. Ask: which step do you usually skip? Take one answer."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Role-play: meet Nordvia's CIO","min":65,"where":"Breakout rooms of three plus one senior guest · Practice 4.2","b":["Plan: Kelvar renewal, devices to Group framework, Brightline rate card","Three rounds of 20 minutes; rotate presenter, observer, stakeholder","Guest plays Maria Lenz, CIO, and has a hidden agenda","Observer ticks the checklist; guest gives 3 minutes of feedback","Presenter drafts the decision record"],"note":"Brief in three minutes: roles rotate each round so everyone presents once; guests play the CIO and rotate rooms between rounds. Do not reveal the hidden agenda; say only that the CIO knows something the plan does not. Open rooms for three rounds of 20 minutes and broadcast a two-minute warning in each round. Visit rooms silently; if a room is stuck, whisper to the guest to drop a small hint about 2027 plans."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l3","note":"Run this right after the rooms close, before people relax. Show results and ask a room that found it early: what question unlocked it? Usually it was a question about the next 18 months, not a better slide. Two minutes, then debrief."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Why do hidden agendas stay hidden?","q":"\"If the CIO's plan stayed hidden, it was the presenter's fault, not hers.\"","b":["What did presenters do that kept her quiet?","Which question would have opened it?","Guests: what made you want to share?"],"min":8,"format":"Open floor, guests first","note":"Ask one guest to answer first: what made you want to share, or keep quiet? Then open the floor. Expect the pattern: presenters who pitched for 15 minutes never asked; those who opened with her goals and asked 'has anything changed?' heard about the cloud migration. Keep it constructive, the point is listening, not blaming. Eight minutes."},{"k":"example","h":"What the reveal changes","tag":"Case 4 · after the hidden agenda","p":"The CIO plans a cloud migration during 2027.","b":["Northbyte: no long lock-in; shorter term with exit support","Kelvar: volumes may fall; negotiate flexibility, not commitment","Brightline: migration needs consultants; rate card becomes urgent","The plan got better because someone asked"],"note":"Reveal the hidden agenda for those who did not find it. Walk through each change in one sentence and stress that adjusting the plan in the meeting is a strength, not a loss of face. Ask presenters: did your decision record reflect this? Then send everyone to the break. Four minutes."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 11:05. Bring your three hardest objections from Fieldwork 3.","note":"Keep this slide on screen with a visible timer. Thank the guests and confirm which room each guest will run in the carousel. Remind people to return on time; the carousel needs everyone present to rotate."},{"k":"section","n":"4.3","h":"Objection carousel","sub":"Hear the objection, then make it work for them","note":"Welcome people back and check everyone is in. Say: objections are information, not attacks. One minute."},{"k":"steps","h":"Answer in four steps","s":[["Acknowledge","Show you heard it, without arguing"],["Explore","Ask why; find the interest"],["Reframe","Link the answer to their goal"],["Propose","Offer a small, concrete next step"]],"note":"Recap in 90 seconds. The common mistake is jumping from objection straight to reframe and sounding defensive; the explore step is where the real reason appears. Model it quickly with 'Procurement slows us down': acknowledge, then ask what their real deadline is. Move to the carousel."},{"k":"exercise","h":"The carousel: five rooms, five objections","min":35,"where":"Five breakout rooms, run by guests and the teacher · Practice 4.3","b":["Chosen supplier · Slows us down · My budget","Savings not real · Group framework does not fit","Pairs rotate rooms every 7 minutes","Answer in four steps; the host pushes back once","Note the best line you hear in each room"],"note":"Assign each guest one objection room and take one yourself. Pairs rotate every 7 minutes so each pair visits all five; use the breakout timer and broadcast 'rotate now'. Ask hosts to push back once on every answer, as a real stakeholder would. Participants should leave with a four-step answer for each of their three objections in Practice 4.3."},{"k":"discuss","h":"When the objection is right","q":"Which objection is actually right about us?","b":["\"Procurement slows us down\": when was it true?","\"Your savings are not real\": can we prove them?","What would we have to change first?"],"min":7,"format":"Chat waterfall, then open floor","note":"Ask everyone to type the objection they think is most deserved and send on your signal. Read the cluster and pick the most common one. Ask: what would we need to change in our own process so this objection disappears? This is an honest moment; do not defend the function, collect two concrete fixes and move on. Seven minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Stakeholder panel","q":"What would make you call Procurement first?","b":["When did Procurement last surprise you positively?","What makes you avoid us?","What should we stop doing tomorrow?","Questions from the room"],"min":20,"format":"Panel Q&A","note":"This is the emotional peak of the programme. Ask the guests the headline question first, one answer each, then the prompts. Open to the room after about 8 minutes; invite questions in the chat and read them out so the panel stays on camera. Capture three quotes on screen to reuse in the close. Thank the panel warmly."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 12:20. Open your diary for the next 12 months.","note":"Keep the timer on screen. Ask people to come back with their calendar open: the next block designs real review dates with their stakeholder. Use the break to note the panel quotes for the closing slide."},{"k":"section","n":"4.4","h":"Governance that keeps you ahead","sub":"Early involvement needs a routine both sides own","note":"Say that a great validation meeting fades in three months without a routine. Ask: how many of you meet your priority stakeholder outside a live request today? Hands up. One minute."},{"k":"table","h":"Rhythm by segment","t":[["Segment","Rhythm","Content"],["Strategic partner","Quarterly review, monthly pipeline","Plan, radar, supplier performance"],["Develop","Every two months","Pipeline, one insight, quick wins"],["Maintain","Twice a year","Renewals and changes"],["Serve efficiently","Once a year","Catalogue and frameworks"]],"note":"Link back to the segmentation from Session 1. Point out that not everyone gets a quarterly review; effort follows value. Ask one person to say which segment their priority stakeholder is in and what rhythm they would propose. Three minutes."},{"k":"cards","h":"Three tools to see demand early","c":[["Demand radar","Rolling 18 months of projects, renewals and budget changes"],["Early-warning triggers","Budget approval, project kick-off, 12 months before expiry"],["One sourcing list","Every initiative in the team list, reviewed weekly"]],"note":"Explain each card in one line. Use Nordvia: the Orbis contract ends in March 2027, so the 12-month trigger should already have fired. Ask: which trigger would have saved you from your last urgent renewal? Take one example. Remind them most of the pipeline comes from expiring contracts, so the radar is mostly a contract-dates exercise. Three minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Reviews: value or ritual?","q":"\"A business review with nothing new is worse than no review.\"","b":["What must Procurement bring every time?","Who owns the agenda: us or them?","How do you rescue a review that has gone stale?"],"min":8,"format":"Agree / disagree, then pairs","note":"Take a quick thumbs-up or down vote, then give pairs two minutes to agree one thing Procurement must bring to every review. Collect answers in the chat: typical ones are one market insight, the radar, supplier performance and a decision needed. Then ask everyone to fill Practice 4.4 for 5 minutes: rhythm, first three dates and two triggers. Eight minutes plus the writing time."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Becoming the person they invite","q":"Think of a colleague everyone invites early. What do they do?","b":["Small, repeated behaviours, not big gestures","Fast on small things, early on bad news","Insight unasked; credit to the stakeholder"],"min":6,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask people to type one behaviour of the person they are thinking of, and send together. Read them and map them to the list from the video: respond fast, bring insight, speak business language, keep promises, give credit, deliver a quick win. Point out that none of these needs authority or budget. Six minutes, then the poll."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l4","note":"Launch the poll and show the spread. Say that whatever they chose becomes Start 1 on their commitment card in Practice 4.5: three behaviours to start and one to stop. Give them 4 minutes to write it, then ask them to post their 'stop' in the chat. Their trio will check the card at the capstone."},{"k":"steps","h":"Your next 90 days","s":[["This week","Commitment card shared with your trio"],["Within 4 weeks","Validation meeting with your stakeholder"],["48 hours later","Decision record sent"],["Same week","First business review booked"],["Before the capstone","One quick win and the five partnership questions"],["Capstone","10 minutes plus 5 of questions to a panel"]],"note":"Brief the capstone: about four weeks from today, online, to two senior internal clients, the Head of Procurement and their line manager. Repeat the rule clearly: a plan the stakeholder has not seen does not pass; the decision record is required. Mention the weights: business alignment and strategy quality count most. Ask anyone who has not booked their validation meeting to do it before the end of the week. Four minutes."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Four sessions, one way of working","b":["WHO: invest where stakeholders matter most","WHAT is happening: tell a Spend Story in their language","WHAT to propose: options and a point of view","HOW: validate together, then keep a rhythm","Be the person they invite first"],"note":"Close the course by walking the four questions of the programme in one sentence each. Read one or two of the panel quotes you captured. Ask everyone to type in the chat one thing they will do differently on Monday, and read a few aloud. Thank the guests, the participants and the Group sponsors, and remind them of the capstone date and where to find the workbook and templates. End on time."}],"polls":[{"id":"s4-l1","q":"Fieldwork 3: did you pre-align your plan with an ally?","options":["Yes, and it changed the plan","Yes, but nothing changed","I tried, they had no time","Not yet"]},{"id":"s4-l2","q":"Which trust term is weakest with your priority stakeholder?","options":["Expertise: they doubt we know their world","Reliability: we have missed promises","Closeness: they do not share plans with us","Self-orientation: they think we chase savings"]},{"id":"s4-l3","q":"In your role-play round, was the CIO's hidden agenda discovered?","options":["Yes, in the first 15 minutes","Yes, but late","Only after a hint","No"]},{"id":"s4-l4","q":"Which behaviour will you start first?","options":["Bring market insight unasked","Respond fast to small things","Deliver a visible quick win","Give the stakeholder credit","Say early when I cannot deliver"]}]}